###############################################################################
# 9c. SESSION INTENT
#
# The other half of the adaptive tier, and the one that needs no data at all.
# "How hard do you want this session to be?" is a DIRECTIVE, where readiness is
# a MEASUREMENT -- so it needs no baseline, works on install day, and is not
# health data. It is the control a real UI would put in front of the user;
# recovery_history is the optional enrichment behind it.
#
#   light 0.85  |  moderate 1.00 (default)  |  challenging 1.15
#
# The two adaptive inputs MULTIPLY, and readiness additionally CAPS the result
# at 1.0 whenever it binds -- see the fourth request, which is the case plain
# multiplication gets wrong.
#
# Session length is held in every case. "Harder" means more total work in the
# same window, not a longer workout and not a heavier top set -- the engine
# solves load from volume and has no 1RM model yet.
###############################################################################

### LIGHT -- 15% less work, same session length.
#
# Observed: load_multiplier = 0.85. 4x8@80 -> 3x12@60.0.
# Volume 2560 -> 2160, time 456 -> 453. dose_outcome = varied_for_session_intent.
POST {{host}}/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "routine": {
    "name": "Bench day",
    "entries": [
      {
        "exercise_id": "barbell-bench-press",
        "dose": {
          "kind": "reps",
          "sets": 4,
          "reps": 8,
          "weight": 80
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "context": {
    "session_intent": "light"
  },
  "seed": 3,
  "substitution_prob": 0
}

### MODERATE -- the default, and exactly the old volume-preserving behaviour.
#
# This must compose to a no-op, or every caller that never surfaces the control
# silently changes behaviour.
#
# Observed: load_multiplier = 1.0. 4x8@80 -> 3x12@70.0.
# Volume 2560 -> 2520 (within the 7.5% tolerance), dose_outcome = varied.
POST {{host}}/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "routine": {
    "name": "Bench day",
    "entries": [
      {
        "exercise_id": "barbell-bench-press",
        "dose": {
          "kind": "reps",
          "sets": 4,
          "reps": 8,
          "weight": 80
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "context": {
    "session_intent": "moderate"
  },
  "seed": 3,
  "substitution_prob": 0
}

### CHALLENGING -- 15% more work, still the same session length.
#
# Readiness is never allowed to raise a target on its own; an explicit user
# directive is. The band is deliberately narrow: a dial wide enough to
# self-prescribe a 50% jump would quietly become a progression mechanism with
# none of periodization's safeguards.
#
# Observed: load_multiplier = 1.15. 4x8@80 -> 3x12@82.5.
# Volume 2560 -> 2970, time 456 -> 453. dose_outcome = varied_for_session_intent.
POST {{host}}/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "routine": {
    "name": "Bench day",
    "entries": [
      {
        "exercise_id": "barbell-bench-press",
        "dose": {
          "kind": "reps",
          "sets": 4,
          "reps": 8,
          "weight": 80
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "context": {
    "session_intent": "challenging"
  },
  "seed": 3,
  "substitution_prob": 0
}

### THE CAP: challenging + suppressed HRV. Read this one carefully.
#
# 1.15 x 0.90 = 1.035, which would hand an under-recovered user MORE work than
# their normal prescription -- precisely what the adaptive tier exists to
# prevent. So a binding readiness caps the composed multiplier at 1.0.
#
# Note what this looks like in the response, because it is a trap: the dose is
# genuinely volume-preserving, so every entry honestly reports
# dose_outcome = varied, NOT varied_for_strain. The user asked for a hard
# session and got a normal one, and the only thing that says so is the
# combination of session_intent = challenging with readiness.state = suppressed
# and load_multiplier = 1.0. (`RoutineVariation.intent_capped_by_readiness`
# answers this directly in-process.)
#
# Observed: load_multiplier = 1.0, readiness.state = suppressed.
# 4x8@80 -> 3x12@70.0, identical to the MODERATE request above.
POST {{host}}/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "routine": {
    "name": "Bench day",
    "entries": [
