### (d) THE TAPER: a meet in seven days, against the steady block from (a).
#
# Linear from 1.0 at fourteen days out to 0.55 on the day, so seven days out is
# 0.775. Send this with event_date pushed to 2026-08-24 (14 days) and
# 2026-08-10 (today) to see the ramp; push it to 2026-10-01 and the plan is
# still reported -- "52 days away, outside the 14-day taper window" -- because
# silence would read as the feature being missing.
#
# Observed: taper.load_multiplier 0.775, binding_taper true, workload still
# "optimal" and visible in `explain` even though it did not bind.
# 4x8@80 -> 3x12@55.0, volume 2560 -> 1980, time 456 -> 453.
#
# KNOWN LIMIT, and it matters here more than anywhere else in this file: a real
# taper cuts volume and HOLDS intensity, so a lifter keeps touching near-maximal
# loads on less total work. This engine solves weight from a volume target, so
# the cut comes out as a lighter bar instead. Right shape for a strain deload,
# wrong shape for peaking. See HANDOFF.md §5.
POST {{host}}/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "routine": {
    "name": "Bench day",
    "entries": [
      {"exercise_id": "barbell-bench-press", "dose": {"kind": "reps", "sets": 4, "reps": 8, "weight": 80}}
    ]
  },
