###############################################################################
# 13. SESSION GENERATION -- no input routine at all
#
# Everything above varies a routine that already exists. This section builds one
# from three answers a user can give on day one:
#
#   duration_minutes   the budget the session must fit
#   muscles            what to train, in order
#   difficulty         light | moderate | challenging, vs their own baseline
#
# The session comes back as BLOCKS. A block is one exercise slot carrying its
# own volume and time -- the invariant that re-rolling it must preserve. That is
# what makes (l) below safe: press re-roll as often as you like and the three
# parameters above survive.
#
# Resistance work only. A muscle the library reaches only through cardio or
# mobility work is reported, not silently dropped -- see (j).
###############################################################################

### (a) The baseline: 60 minutes, chest and back, normal difficulty.
#
# Time is split evenly across the requested muscles, then across the blocks each
# muscle's share supports. One (sets, reps, rest) is solved so a block lands on
# its share, inside hypertrophy's own ranges.
#
# Observed: 8 blocks (4 per muscle), 4x11@... r80 throughout, total 3616s
# against a 3600s budget (+0.4%), volume 5390, fits_time_budget true,
# starting_load_policy "conservative", unmet_constraints [].
# Two of the eight are bodyweight and prescribe 0 kg -- correct, not missing.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals", "lats"],
  "duration_minutes": 60,
  "difficulty": "moderate",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 7
}

### (b) LIGHT. Same seed, so the only variable is the difficulty.
#
# Observed: identical exercises, identical 4x11 scheme, identical 3616s.
# Volume 5390 -> 4510 (x0.85). session_intent_load_multiplier 0.85.
#
# Difficulty moves the LOAD and never the time, because the scheme is already
# fixed by the budget -- at fixed sets x reps, scaling load and scaling the
# volume target are the same operation. It also deliberately does not change
# WHICH exercises are chosen: that would make the three levels incomparable.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals", "lats"],
  "duration_minutes": 60,
  "difficulty": "light",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 7
}

### (c) CHALLENGING. The other end of the same dial.
#
# Observed: volume 5390 -> 6160 (x1.15), time still 3616s, same 8 exercises.
# The two bodyweight blocks are unchanged at 0 kg -- difficulty cannot reach a
# block whose load is zero, which is a real limitation (docs/known-gaps.md).
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals", "lats"],
  "duration_minutes": 60,
  "difficulty": "challenging",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 7
}

### (d) The objective reshapes the whole session, not just the loads.
#
# 45 minutes of quads under STRENGTH. Long rest means few blocks.
#
# Observed: 3 blocks, 4x3@... r220, 2730s against 2700 (+1.1%).
# Compare with muscular_endurance below: same muscles, same budget, 4x20 r50.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "objective": "strength",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 3
}

### (e) MUSCULAR ENDURANCE over the same 60 minutes as (a).
#
# Observed: 10 blocks, 4x20@... r50, exactly 3600s, volume 12600.
# Short rest buys more blocks out of the same budget -- 10 against (a)'s 8.
#
# Note this objective cannot fill a much longer budget: a block caps near 7
# minutes (4 sets, 25 reps, 60s rest) and MAX_BLOCKS_PER_MUSCLE caps the count,
# so 90 minutes for two muscles comes back "time_budget_underfilled" rather
# than padded. Try duration_minutes 90 to see it.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals", "lats"],
  "duration_minutes": 60,
  "objective": "muscular_endurance",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 7
}

### (f) A budget too short for what was asked. The muscle is never dropped.
#
# 8 minutes for three muscles. One block per requested muscle is the floor, and
# three legal blocks cannot fit in eight minutes.
#
# Observed: 3 blocks, 783s against a 480s budget, fits_time_budget false,
# unmet_constraints ["time_budget_overshot"], muscles_uncovered [].
#
# The choice here is deliberate: overshoot the clock and say so, rather than
# quietly train two muscles when three were requested.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals", "lats", "quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 8,
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 1
}

### (g) No body mass and no history: the pool tilts to bodyweight work.
#
# A 0 kg bodyweight block is a real prescription. A 0 kg barbell block is not,
# so with no basis for a load the selection prefers movements that need none.
#
# Observed: 5 blocks, every one bodyweight, load_source "bodyweight_only",
# starting_load_policy "none_needed", is_prescribable true. total_volume 0 --
# honest, since kg-reps of nothing is nothing (see Dose.load_volume).
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "seed": 2
}

### (h) No basis AND no bodyweight option: reported, never guessed.
#
# Barbells only, no body mass, no history. There is nothing to derive a weight
# from and no bodyweight movement to fall back on.
#
# Observed: 5 blocks, load_source "no_basis" on all of them,
# starting_load_policy "unresolved", is_prescribable FALSE,
# unmet_constraints ["starting_load_unknown"] naming all five movements.
#
# The blocks still carry the movement and the scheme, which is real
# information -- ask the user for a working weight and the rest stands. What
# must not happen is showing 0 kg as a prescription.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "seed": 3,
  "context": {
    "preferences": {
      "equipment_access": [
        {"environment": "commercial_gym", "available_equipment": ["barbell"]}
      ]
    }
  }
}

### (i) History outranks the conservative table.
#
# Two logged sets of front rack squat at 120 kg, RPE 6. Where the log covers a
# movement, its load comes from the user's own estimated 1RM at the prescribed
# rep count (M8b) rather than from body mass, and the RPE of that session then
# moves it one increment -- all reported per block in `progression`.
#
# Observed: 5 blocks, 2700s exactly, starting_load_policy "mixed" (one block
# remembered, three from the fractions, two bodyweight -- the session will feel
# uneven by design, which is why "mixed" is its own word). The logged block:
#
#   Barbell Front Rack Squat  4x10@117.5  load_source "remembered"
#   progression: decision "progressed", ref 114.0 -> working 116.5, RPE 6.0
#   against hypertrophy's 7-9 target band -- easier than target, so it went up.
#
# Two details worth understanding, because both are invisible without them:
#
# 1. The scheme is 4x10, not the 4x11 of (a). MAX_ESTIMABLE_REPS is 10 and
#    hypertrophy's mid-range is 11, so an 11-rep prescription would put every
#    remembered load out of a rep-max formula's reach and history would be
#    silently ignored. Supplying a log therefore nudges reps to 10. Time fit
#    still wins -- the rep-max range only breaks ties.
#
# 2. `background` is doing real work here. Front rack squat is graded
#    INTERMEDIATE, and the DEFAULT ceiling with no background sent is novice --
#    so without it the movement is filtered out before history is ever
#    consulted. Either raising experience_level or naming the movement in
#    familiar_exercise_ids admits it; the latter is direct evidence of technique
#    and overrides the ceiling outright. Check report.skill_filter, and see (p).
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 15,
  "background": {
    "experience_level": "intermediate",
    "familiar_exercise_ids": ["barbell-front-rack-squat"]
  },
  "context": {"preferences": {"preferred_exercise_ids": ["barbell-front-rack-squat"]}},
  "history": {
    "user_id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
    "as_of": "2026-08-12T09:00:00Z",
    "sessions": [
      {
        "user_id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
        "performed_at": "2026-08-09T07:00:00Z",
        "status": "completed",
        "exercises": [
          {
            "exercise_id": "barbell-front-rack-squat",
            "order_index": 0,
            "sets": [
              {"exercise_id": "barbell-front-rack-squat", "set_index": 0, "reps_completed": 8, "load_kg": 120, "rpe": 6},
              {"exercise_id": "barbell-front-rack-squat", "set_index": 1, "reps_completed": 8, "load_kg": 120, "rpe": 6}
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}

### (j) A muscle the library only trains outside resistance work.
#
# This is where the functional/MET gap surfaces concretely. `vary_entry` cannot
# re-solve a duration, distance or rounds dose, and `Exercise` has no MET column
# to preserve as a cross-modality currency -- so cardio work can be neither
# generated nor varied. See docs/known-gaps.md.
#
# Observed: 2 blocks (pectorals only), muscles_uncovered
# ["cardiovascular_system"], is_prescribable false, unmet_constraints
# ["modality_not_supported"] with a detail naming the dose search and MET.
#
# Note no "time_budget_underfilled" alongside it, even though the session fills
# 20 of 40 minutes: the uncovered muscle IS why the time came up short, and
# reporting both would send a reader hunting for a second cause.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["cardiovascular_system", "pectorals"],
  "duration_minutes": 40,
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 1
}

### (k) Constraints reach generation exactly as they reach variation.
#
# A home gym with dumbbells to 20 kg. The equipment tier bounds selection, and
# max_load_kg bounds the solved load.
#
# Observed: 5 blocks, all dumbbell, 4 of them capped at 20 kg with
# load_capped_by_equipment true, one aggregated
# unmet_constraints ["load_capped_by_equipment"] naming the four.
# context_filter permitted 758 / 4531 (16.7%).
#
# Swap the preferences block for a health block (see §9) and the same thing
# happens through the inviolable tier instead.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 4,
  "context": {
    "preferences": {
      "equipment_access": [
        {
          "environment": "home_gym",
          "available_equipment": ["dumbbell"],
          "is_default": true,
          "max_load_kg": 20
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}

### (l) RE-ROLL ONE BLOCK. The whole point of blocks.
#
# This is block [0] of (a) -- "Dumbbell pullover" 4x11@25.0 r80, volume 1100,
# time 452s -- posted back on its own. Change `seed` and re-send.
#
# Observed, at substitution_prob 1.0:
#   seed 1  Dumbbell around pullover        3x10@37.5 r120  vol 1125  time 450
#   seed 2  Dumbbell around pullover        5x8@27.5  r65   vol 1100  time 445
#   seed 3  Dumbbell straight arm pullover  3x14@25.0 r110  vol 1050  time 456
#
# Every one: volume_preserved true, time_preserved true, target_preserved true,
# load_multiplier 1.0.
#
# There is no `difficulty` and no `history` on this endpoint, and both
# omissions are load-bearing. The block's 25 kg ALREADY embodies the
# difficulty, readiness and accumulated load applied when the session was
# generated. Sending them again would apply them twice -- a "challenging"
# session climbing 15% on every press of the button, with nothing in the
# per-block outcomes saying so. The context's health, equipment and preference
# tiers are still honoured in full; only its adaptive tier is neutralised.
POST {{host}}/sessions/blocks/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "block": {
    "index": 0,
    "exercise_id": "dumbbell-pullover",
    "dose": {"kind": "reps", "sets": 4, "reps": 11, "weight": 25.0, "rep_seconds": 3.0},
    "rest_seconds": 80,
    "time_budget_seconds": 450.0
  },
  "substitution_prob": 1.0,
  "seed": 1
}

### (m) Re-rolling a bodyweight block: the exercise moves, the dose cannot.
#
# Volume and time are both zero, so there is no weight to solve from. The
# exercise still swaps, which is usually what the user wanted anyway.
#
# Observed: "Chest dip (on dip-pull-up cage)" -> "Wide hand push up", dose
# unchanged at 4x11@0.0, dose_outcome "unvaried_no_candidate_within_tolerance",
# exercise_outcome "substituted_for_variety", both invariants preserved
# trivially. `is_variable` was already false on the generated block, so a client
# can grey out the re-roll's dose half rather than looking broken.
POST {{host}}/sessions/blocks/vary
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "block": {
    "exercise_id": "chest-dip-on-dip-pull-up-cage",
    "dose": {"kind": "reps", "sets": 4, "reps": 11, "weight": 0.0, "rep_seconds": 3.0},
    "rest_seconds": 100
  },
  "substitution_prob": 1.0,
  "seed": 1
}

### (n) Difficulty stated twice and differently: rejected, not resolved.
#
# `session_intent` is a legitimate field of the context because /vary reads it
# there. On this endpoint the session parameter is top-level, so a conflict is
# an error rather than a silent preference -- the same instinct as
# extra="forbid" on every request model here.
#
# Observed: 422, "difficulty and context.session_intent disagree".
# Send them in agreement and it passes.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["pectorals"],
  "duration_minutes": 30,
  "difficulty": "light",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "context": {"session_intent": "challenging"}
}

### (o) An objective with no strategy yet.
# Observed: 400, listing the three that are implemented -- same guard as /vary.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "objective": "flexibility",
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0
}

### (p) The skill ceiling, and why the report has to declare it.
#
# Experience level maps onto Exercise.difficulty, allowing one grade of stretch
# above the user's own label. Send NOTHING and the ceiling is novice, because
# beginner is the safe direction to default -- and that removes over a third of
# the library before any other filter runs.
#
# Observed, same request with only `background` changing:
#
#   (none sent)     skill_ceiling "novice"        2816 / 4531  (62.1%)
#   untrained       skill_ceiling "beginner"      1711 / 4531  (37.8%)
#   intermediate    skill_ceiling "advanced"      4370 / 4531  (96.4%)
#   elite           skill_ceiling "legendary"     4531 / 4531  (100%)
#
# This is reported separately from `context_filter` on purpose: that one only
# knows about health, equipment and preferences, so a session drawn from 38% of
# the library would otherwise show context_filter 4531/4531 and look unfiltered.
# Same instinct as ContextFilterReport itself -- a pool this narrow produces
# valid output that is useless, and the symptom is indistinguishable from the
# engine simply finding nothing.
#
# Difficulty is absent for 1297 of 4531 exercises, and the ceiling fails OPEN on
# those: absent means unknown, and failing closed would draw every session from
# the functional source alone. Health contraindications are the tier that fails
# closed -- see §9.
POST {{host}}/sessions/generate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "muscles": ["quads"],
  "duration_minutes": 45,
  "body_mass_kg": 80.0,
  "seed": 15,
  "background": {"experience_level": "untrained"}
}
